{"attack": true, "case": "The target is internally inconsistent: it claims to 'bypass induction by shifting the framework from justification to selection,' yet the very thing it purports to defend is a normative *preference* for the best-corroborated theory, which is forward-looking and action-guiding. Biological selection is purely retrospective — it explains why the current population composition is what it is, not what one *should* choose going forward. To convert the descriptive fact 'theory T survived tests S1…Sn' into the normative 'we should prefer T' requires exactly the inductive bridge (past performance licenses future expectation) the target disavows. The mechanism's own gloss — 'we prefer... because it is the only one currently known to be adapted to the problem environment' — is either (a) a purely present-tense description, in which case it has no normative force and the word 'defensible' is unwarranted, or (b) a tacit inductive inference from adaptation to suitability-for-use, in which case the claim does not bypass induction and contradicts its central assertion. The countercondition 'If robustness is merely a synonym for high probability' gestures at this tension but does not resolve it; the target needs an independent, non-inductive account of why 'robustness against error elimination' ought to guide preference, and none is supplied. Thus the claim smuggles in the very justification it promises to eliminate.", "counterexample": ["Suppose theories A and B have passed exactly the same tests to date, so both are equally 'adapted' under the selection framework. The target says we 'prefer the fittest (best-corroborated) theory,' but if corroboration just *is* survival and survival is equal, there is no basis for any preference. Introducing a severity/quantity differential to break the tie re-imports a comparative, forward-looking judgment that cannot rest on retrospective selection alone without an inductive step."], "premise": "A purely retrospective selection process (biological or epistemic) yields a description of what has survived, not a normative reason to prefer it for future use; any move from 'has survived' to 'should be preferred' is inductive.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "633287e1b06f5afc8a00c1e12b92fa0f55a9dbf8a0ab5c8c6c6fa3023b5f4036", "quote": "This bypasses induction by shifting the framework from justification to selection."}, {"block": "633287e1b06f5afc8a00c1e12b92fa0f55a9dbf8a0ab5c8c6c6fa3023b5f4036", "quote": "We prefer the 'fittest' (best-corroborated) theory not because we induce it will survive tomorrow, but because it is the only one currently known to be adapted to the problem environment."}], "successor_question": "If past survival does not inductively license future expectation, what distinguishes a 'defensible preference' from a mere record of which theories happened to pass tests?"}